# Test: the looking glass API and its route table answer how long ago a route
#       last changed
# QUESTION: is the birdwatcher `age` of a route the time since bgp-rib last
#           stored a change to it, and never a constant, and does the route
#           table show the same age?
# VALIDATES: against the real bgp-rib, with 10.50.1.0/24 stored once,
#            GET /api/looking-glass/routes/prefix (transformRoutes,
#            internal/component/lg/handler_api.go) answers an `age` that
#            grows to 4 seconds and more
#            (spec-lg-peer-history-origin-search-and-named-nodes AC-7). The
#            search page (routeResults, route_table.templ) lists that route
#            with an Age cell whose data-seconds lies between the API's answer
#            before the page was read and its answer after (AC-7). The
#            same route announced again with equal attributes keeps an age at
#            least as large, and the route announced with another AS path
#            answers an age no larger than the seconds since that change
#            (AC-8).
# PREVENTS: a mandatory API field that always reads zero, which a client
#           cannot tell from a route learned this second, and an age that a
#           route refresh resets, which makes a stable route look new.
#
# WHY A FUNCTIONAL TEST: the looking glass copies `age` from the row bgp-rib
# writes, and its package tests feed it a row written by hand. Only the real
# plugin proves that a stored route carries a time and that the row holds it
# under the key the transform reads.
#
# NO SLEEP: the fixture (internal/test/fixture/lg_route_age.go) reads the API
# until the age reaches 4, a bounded poll over the value under test. The two
# verdicts after it compare ages with each other and with the time the fixture
# measured around its own change, so a slow machine cannot turn a kept age
# into a restarted one: a run too slow to tell them apart fails saying so.
#
# NOT COVERED HERE: the wording of the Age cell ("3m 12s"), which
# TestFormatElapsed owns, and a row with no age, which bgp-rib writes only for
# a sent route or a best path row (TestRouteRowsLeaveAMissingAgeEmpty,
# TestTransformRoutesAgeAvailability, internal/component/lg).

# ze-peer expectations (minimal handshake)
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=50s
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
EOF_PEER

# Pass the looking-glass listen port ($PORT2) to the plugin process.
option=env:var=ZE_LG_PORT:value=$PORT2

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external lg-route-age {
		run "le test fixture plugin/lg-route-age"
		encoder json
	}
	internal bgp-rib {
		use bgp-rib
	}
}

environment {
	looking-glass {
		enabled true
		# This test asserts looking-glass CONTENT over plain HTTP, so it takes
		# the documented TLS opt-out.
		tls false
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1
			port $PORT2
		}
	}
}

bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65000
			}
			router-id 10.0.0.1
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}

		attach process bgp-rib {
			receive [ update state refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=45s
expect=exit:code=0
# The fixture prints this only after the three ages read as expected; a failed
# read returns an error, which the runner reads as the test's failure.
expect=stderr:pattern=OK: the API age of 10\.50\.1\.0/24 grew to
# AC-7, the page half: printed only after the Age cell's seconds were read
# between two API answers.
expect=stderr:pattern=OK: the route table showed 10\.50\.1\.0/24 as
